WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
A crew is halfway through a roof inspection when the battery warning appears earlier than expected.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.280
The client asks for one more pass.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:15.180
The aircraft is downwind of the launch point, people are walking near the safest landing area, and the pilot is tired from working in heat.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.920
The emergency did not begin with a flashing warning.

5
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:22.820
It began when pressure, assumptions, and reduced margins started to accumulate.

6
00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.600
Good emergency management starts while the flight still feels normal and options remain available.

7
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:38.160
Aeronautical decision-making is the habit of identifying hazards, assessing risk, applying mitigations, and deciding whether residual risk is acceptable.

8
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:43.780
PAVE helps organize risk around Pilot, Aircraft, enVironment, and External pressures.

9
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:51.880
IMSAFE checks illness, medication, stress, alcohol, fatigue, and emotion or eating.

10
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.620
These acronyms support judgment, but they do not replace judgment.

11
00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.880
A pilot can recite them and still rationalize a bad launch if commercial pressure is high enough.

12
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.920
Human factors are predictable.

13
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.580
Anti-authority says the rule is unnecessary.

14
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.240
Impulsivity says act before thinking.

15
00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.460
Invulnerability says the accident happens to someone else.

16
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.840
Machismo says the pilot can prove skill by pressing on.

17
00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.460
Resignation says there is nothing useful to do.

18
00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.880
Confirmation bias favors the information that supports the desired flight.

19
00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.460
Plan-continuation bias keeps a mission alive after the facts have changed.

20
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:33.720
Normalization of deviance makes yesterday's shortcut feel like today's procedure.

21
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.720
Emergency planning should be done before takeoff.

22
00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:58.100
The crew should define abort criteria for unexpected aircraft, loss of visual line of sight, a person or vehicle entering the area, weather deterioration, rapid battery depletion, control-link warnings, GPS degradation, compass anomalies, navigation errors, sensor failures, flyaway behavior, propulsion problems, or payload movement.

23
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.320
The response depends on the actual hazard.

24
00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.060
Hovering may buy time if the airspace is clear.

25
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:09.040
Return-to-home may be wrong if its path crosses people, obstacles, or a headwind.

26
00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:14.500
A controlled landing at a nearby alternate site may be better than forcing a return to the launch point.

27
00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:23.500
Part 107 gives the remote pilot in command emergency authority to deviate from a Part 107 rule to the extent necessary to meet an in-flight emergency.

28
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.960
That authority is not blanket permission to ignore planning.

29
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.540
It is a narrow tool for immediate safety.

30
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:35.160
After a deviation, the pilot must provide a written report to the FAA if requested.

31
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:49.580
The pilot should also secure the scene, assist injured people without creating additional hazards, isolate damaged batteries, preserve flight records, document decisions, and prevent a damaged aircraft from being relaunched casually.

32
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:56.500
Certain safety events must be reported to the FAA no later than 10 calendar days after the operation.

33
00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:14.040
The threshold includes serious injury to any person, any loss of consciousness, or damage to property other than the small unmanned aircraft when the cost of repair, including materials and labor, exceeds $500 or the fair market value exceeds $500 in a total loss.

34
00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:21.260
In the roof scenario, the safe answer is to reject the one more pass request and recover the aircraft with margin.

35
00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.480
The operational takeaway is that emergency skill starts with preflight honesty.
